  "identity": {
    "playing_style": "Under Luis Enrique PSG play a possession-heavy 4-3-3 with an aggressive counter-press, technical midfield (Vitinha, Joao Neves, Fabian Ruiz) and interchanging forwards rather than a fixed striker. The side that won back-to-back Champions Leagues (2024/25, 2025/26) is built on collective pressing and young, mobile attackers; the 2026 signing of Ferran Torres and Akliouche continues the preference for versatile forwards over a classic target man.",
    "principles": [
      "post-Galactico collective project (since 2023)",
      "young French/European talent with resale value",
      "sell surplus attackers at premium fees (Ramos, Lee, Kolo Muani, Barcola in 2026)",
      "coach-led sporting model (Luis Enrique + Luis Campos)"
    ],
    "club_character": "State-linked ownership with effectively unlimited backing but now operating under UEFA squad-cost rules; enormous media pressure in Paris; Parc des Princes ultras (CUP) vocal; club has pivoted from star signings to a collective identity and is now a reigning double European champion.",
    "ownership": "Qatar Sports Investments (state-linked, sovereign wealth), owner since 2011",
    "political_and_social_context": "Ownership by Qatar Sports Investments has been publicly debated in the context of Qatar's human-rights record and 'sportswashing' criticism; PSG has faced UEFA Financial Fair Play scrutiny (settlements in 2014 and investigations 2017-18). President Nasser Al-Khelaifi also chairs the European Club Association. Long-running public dispute with the city of Paris over ownership of the Parc des Princes and possible stadium relocation.",
    "languages": [
      "fr",
      "es",
      "pt",
      "en"
    ],
    "wage_structure": "hierarchical, but flattened since 2023 after the departure of Messi/Neymar/Mbappe",
    "transfer_policy": "Buy young high-ceiling players (mostly under 25) for large fees, sell surplus attackers at profit; no longer star-driven; Luis Campos as football advisor",
    "injury_management_reputation": "Publicly documented: strong medical/performance staff investment; Ousmane Dembele's muscle injuries and Nuno Mendes/Hakimi absences were recurring stories in 2025/26, but no systemic criticism of the department."
